with q as (
  select $l28_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What did the Cassini spacecraft do at Enceladus that no spacecraft had done before, and why was it possible?", "options": ["It landed on the surface and drilled through the ice to reach the ocean", "It flew directly through the moon's plumes and sampled the chemistry of a subsurface ocean \u2014 possible because Enceladus sprays its hidden ocean into space through geysers at its south pole, so the sea can be tasted at altitude with no landing and no drilling", "It photographed liquid water on the surface for the first time", "It used radar to map the ocean floor from orbit"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Normally, sampling the ocean of an ice-covered world would require landing and drilling through kilometres of ice \u2014 a mission we still cannot fly. But Enceladus vents its subsurface ocean into space through the tiger-stripe fractures at its south pole, so Cassini simply flew through the plumes, again and again, catching the droplets and analysing them. It was, in effect, tasting an alien ocean no human eye had ever seen \u2014 and it found water, salts, organics, and molecular hydrogen."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How did the Galileo spacecraft find evidence for a global ocean inside Europa, and what does Europa's crater-free surface add?", "options": ["It drilled a core sample; the craters show the ice is thin", "It measured an INDUCED magnetic field \u2014 Jupiter's changing field drives currents in a conductive, salty liquid layer inside the moon, which betrays the ocean \u2014 while the near-absence of impact craters shows the surface is young and being resurfaced, as a liquid ocean beneath the ice would do", "It detected the ocean's tides directly; craters are irrelevant", "It saw sunlight glinting off surface water; the craters are frozen lakes"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Jupiter's enormous magnetic field sweeps past Europa and induces electric currents in anything conductive inside it; those currents make a secondary field Galileo detected, and only a global layer of salty (conductive) liquid water explains it \u2014 the ocean announcing itself. Separately, Europa's ice is smooth, cracked, and nearly crater-free, which means it is young and actively resurfaced \u2014 exactly what an ocean cracking and refreezing the shell would produce. Together they point to a global saltwater ocean, holding about twice all of Earth's water, under 10\u201330 km of ice."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What did the 1977 discovery of deep-sea hydrothermal vent ecosystems prove, and why did it transform the search for life on the ocean moons?", "options": ["That life can survive freezing; the moons are too cold anyway", "That life does not need sunlight \u2014 vent ecosystems run on CHEMOSYNTHESIS, harvesting chemical energy from water-rock reactions rather than light \u2014 so a dark, sunless, ice-covered ocean is not a dead end but a potential haven, exactly describing Europa and Enceladus", "That only bacteria can live underwater, so complex life is impossible on the moons", "That deep oceans are lifeless, confirming the moons are too"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Biologists had assumed nearly all life depends ultimately on sunlight (photosynthesis at the base of the food chain). Then in 1977, near the Gal\u00e1pagos, scientists found teeming ecosystems \u2014 giant tube worms, clams, crabs \u2014 thriving in total darkness around vents, powered by chemosynthetic microbes that harvest chemical energy from vent chemistry. Life needs an energy source, not necessarily a star. If the floors of Europa and Enceladus have vents (as the plume hydrogen and hot-water silica suggest), they could power life in the dark just as on Earth \u2014 turning frozen iceballs into premier targets."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why was the molecular hydrogen Cassini found in Enceladus's plumes described as 'not just a sign of hydrothermal activity but food'?", "options": ["Hydrogen is what all life breathes, like oxygen", "Because hot water reacting with rock at the seafloor produces H\u2082 (so it signals hydrothermal vents), AND H\u2082 with the CO\u2082 also present is the exact fuel of methanogens \u2014 some of Earth's most ancient life \u2014 which live by CO\u2082 + 4H\u2082 \u2192 CH\u2084 + 2H\u2082O, the same Sabatier reaction from the life-support chapter run as metabolism", "Hydrogen proves the ocean is warm enough to boil", "Because hydrogen is a greenhouse gas that keeps the ocean liquid"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Hydrogen forms when hot water reacts with rock (serpentinization), so its presence points to hydrothermal vents at Enceladus's ocean floor. But it is more than a signpost: methanogens, among Earth's oldest life, live by combining hydrogen and carbon dioxide to make methane and energy \u2014 CO\u2082 + 4H\u2082 \u2192 CH\u2084 + 2H\u2082O, the very Sabatier reaction used in life support to recover water, here run by cells as their energy source. Enceladus's plumes contain both H\u2082 and CO\u2082, so the exact fuel some of Earth's oldest life eats is present, right now, in that ocean."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is the difference between a world being 'habitable' and being 'inhabited,' and what did Cassini actually establish about Enceladus?", "options": ["They mean the same thing; Cassini proved Enceladus has life", "'Habitable' means life COULD live there (the environment has the conditions); 'inhabited' means life IS there (biology). They are completely different claims \u2014 and Cassini established only that Enceladus's ocean is HABITABLE (it has the ingredients), not that it is inhabited; it measured chemistry, not organisms", "'Habitable' means inhabited by microbes, 'inhabited' means by animals; Cassini found microbes", "'Inhabited' means in the habitable zone; Enceladus is neither"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Habitable is a statement about the environment (it has liquid water, energy, chemistry, nutrients \u2014 life could live here); inhabited is a statement about biology (organisms actually exist here). An uninhabited desert island is habitable without being inhabited. Cassini found Enceladus's ocean HABITABLE \u2014 water, salts, organics, hydrogen, phosphates \u2014 but not INHABITED: it carried no life-detection instruments and found no organisms or definitive biosignatures. The honest statement is that Enceladus has an apparently habitable ocean; whether it harbours life is unknown."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain why the distinction between 'habitable' and 'inhabited' is the honest heart of the ocean-worlds story, and why the gap between the two is 'precisely the gap of the origin of life.' Then explain what makes Enceladus a 'pivotal experiment' rather than merely a beautiful moon \u2014 what each possible outcome of a life-detection mission would tell us.", "rubric": "THE DISTINCTION: HABITABLE = a place COULD support life; it has the necessary CONDITIONS (liquid water, energy, chemistry, nutrients) \u2014 a statement about the ENVIRONMENT. INHABITED = life IS actually there; organisms exist and are living \u2014 a statement about BIOLOGY. These are completely different claims (an uninhabited desert island is habitable without being inhabited). WHAT CASSINI ESTABLISHED: only that Enceladus's ocean appears HABITABLE (water, salts, organics, hydrogen, phosphates \u2014 essentially the full ingredient checklist); it did NOT and COULD NOT establish INHABITED \u2014 it was launched before we knew the ocean existed, carried no life-detection instruments, and measured the plume's CHEMISTRY, not organisms/biosignatures. So the honest statement is: an apparently habitable ocean whose inhabitation is UNKNOWN. WHY THE GAP IS THE ORIGIN-OF-LIFE GAP: having the INGREDIENTS for life is not the same as ORIGINATING life \u2014 the assembly of building blocks into the first living, replicating system is a separate, difficult, still-mysterious step (from the origin-of-life lecture) that may be easy or a near-miracle. Whether a habitable ocean is actually inhabited therefore depends on whether the origin of life is EASY (then the ocean, given hundreds of millions of years, probably came alive \u2014 inhabited) or HARD (then it may hold every ingredient and remain FOREVER STERILE \u2014 habitable but uninhabited). Since we do not know which, we CANNOT infer 'inhabited' from 'habitable': a habitable ocean might teem with life or be completely lifeless, and habitability alone cannot distinguish these. WHY ENCELADUS IS A PIVOTAL EXPERIMENT: because the habitable-to-inhabited gap IS the origin-of-life question, SEARCHING Enceladus's accessible, habitable ocean for actual life is a DIRECT TEST of whether that origin is easy or hard \u2014 the very question that decides whether the universe teems with life or is nearly empty, and whose answer needs a SECOND, INDEPENDENT example of life (N=2, from the what-is-life lecture). THE TWO OUTCOMES: (1) FIND life that arose there independently -> a second independent origin (N=2) -> the origin of life is EASY and the cosmos is full of life. (2) Search thoroughly and find a habitable but LIFELESS ocean -> powerful evidence the origin is HARD/rare (ingredients are not enough). EITHER result would be among the most important discoveries in the history of science \u2014 which is why Enceladus is a ready-made experiment we can actually run, not just a lovely moon, and why the course 'sails to Enceladus.' A strong answer keeps 'habitable' as a genuine, thrilling result (a place worth searching) while insisting it is NOT the discovery of life, and frames the next mission (life-detection instruments flown through the plume) as the way to turn the unknown into an answer to whether we are alone."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Reaching an ocean world's sea, and reading it, requires nearly the whole course. Explain the two ways to sample a subsurface ocean and why Enceladus's gift is decisive; then explain why a spacecraft at Saturn must 'think for itself,' and what planetary protection is and why the Cassini Grand Finale embodied it.", "rubric": "THE TWO WAYS TO SAMPLE A SUBSURFACE OCEAN: (1) DRILL through the ice \u2014 but the deepest hole humans have ever bored, on Earth with all our resources (the Kola borehole), reached only about 12 km after decades, while an ice shell like Europa's is perhaps 10-30 km thick, so drilling through tens of kilometres of ice on a distant moon with a robotic lander is beyond us for the foreseeable future; this would LOCK Europa's ocean away. (2) FLY THROUGH A PLUME \u2014 if the moon sprays its ocean into space (Enceladus), a spacecraft samples the sea at altitude, at flyby speed, with NO landing and NO drilling, catching the droplets as they stream past. WHY ENCELADUS'S GIFT IS DECISIVE: this single difference \u2014 plume-sampling versus ice-drilling \u2014 may be the difference between answering the life question at Enceladus THIS generation and waiting centuries to answer it at Europa; it is why tiny Enceladus, despite Europa's LARGER ocean, may be where we FIRST learn whether an alien ocean is inhabited. (Optional: the rocket equation and gravity assists of the earlier units are what get any payload across ~5-10 AU to these moons in the first place \u2014 Cassini took seven years and multiple flybys just to reach Saturn.) WHY THE PROBE MUST THINK FOR ITSELF: a radio command travels only at the SPEED OF LIGHT, and Saturn is ~9-10 AU away, so a one-way signal takes on the order of an HOUR-plus (each way; round trip longer). A plume flythrough is over in SECONDS \u2014 far faster than any signal can cross the gulf \u2014 so no one on Earth can steer the encounter live; the spacecraft must execute it AUTONOMOUSLY, its decisions programmed in years before the encounter. (This is the deep-space reality: light-speed makes the robot act alone.) PLANETARY PROTECTION: the discipline of not CONTAMINATING a potentially habitable world with Earth life we bring ourselves \u2014 critical for two reasons: (a) ETHICALLY, we could harm or destroy an alien biosphere before ever studying it; (b) SCIENTIFICALLY, our own hitchhiking microbes, if later 'detected,' would be a catastrophic FALSE POSITIVE \u2014 we would mistake Earth life we carried for aliens. So spacecraft bound for such worlds are rigorously sterilized, and missions are sometimes deliberately destroyed to protect what they studied. THE GRAND FINALE: when Cassini ran low on fuel in 2017, controllers deliberately PLUNGED it into Saturn to burn up, specifically so it could never someday crash uncontrolled into Enceladus (or Titan) and seed a pristine, potentially habitable ocean with hitchhiking Earth microbes. The spacecraft that discovered Enceladus's habitable ocean SACRIFICED itself to protect it \u2014 capturing the maturity the search for life demands: to look for life responsibly, we must be willing to protect what we might find, even from ourselves. A strong answer connects these to the whole course \u2014 propulsion and orbital mechanics to get there, autonomy because of light-speed, and an ethic of protection to search responsibly \u2014 all converging on the ocean-world missions."}]$l28_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/space-exploration/astrobiology/ocean-worlds', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
